const GR_P7B=[
 ["Becoming able with ようになる","Verb dictionary or potential form + ようになる","ようになる (you ni naru) says that a person has changed from not being able to do something to being able to do it. Use the potential form or a verb such as 分かる. 泳げるようになりました means “I became able to swim”. For a change in what a person does, use the dictionary form: 毎日走るようになりました means “I started to run every day”.",[["私","watashi","I"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["泳げる","oyogeru","can swim"],["ように","youni","(change marker)"],["なりました","narimashita","became"],["。","",""]],"I have become able to swim."],
 ["Making a habit with ようにする","Verb dictionary form + ようにする","ようにする (you ni suru) means “to try to do something” or “to make a habit of doing something”. Use the dictionary form for something you try to do, and the ない-form for something you try not to do. 毎日歩くようにしています means “I try to walk every day”. 忘れないようにします means “I will take care not to forget”.",[["毎日","mainichi","every day"],["歩く","aruku","walk"],["ように","youni","(aim marker)"],["しています","shite imasu","am trying"],["。","",""]],"I try to walk every day."],
 ["Decisions with ことにする","Dictionary form + ことにする","ことにする (koto ni suru) says that the speaker decides to do something by their own choice. 行くことにしました means “I decided to go”. Use ことにしました for the decision. Use ことにしています for a rule that you keep, for example 毎日歩くことにしています: “I have decided to walk every day, and I do it”.",[["相部屋","aibeya","shared room"],["に","ni","in"],["泊まる","tomaru","stay"],["こと","koto","(act of)"],["に","ni","(result)"],["しました","shimashita","did"],["。","",""]],"I decided to stay in a shared room."],
 ["Arranged events with ことになる","Dictionary form + ことになる","ことになる (koto ni naru) says that something has been decided by other people, by a rule or by the situation, not by the speaker. 日本へ行くことになりました means “It has been decided that I will go to Japan”. Compare ことにする: with ことにする the speaker decides, with ことになる another person or the situation decides.",[["来月","raigetsu","next month"],["海外","kaigai","overseas"],["に","ni","to"],["行く","iku","go"],["こと","koto","(act of)"],["に","ni","(result)"],["なりました","narimashita","became"],["。","",""]],"It has been decided that I will go overseas next month."],
 ["Purpose with ように","Verb dictionary or ない-form + ように, result","ように (you ni) before a comma gives the purpose of the action that follows: “so that”, “in order that”. The verb before ように is usually a potential verb, a verb of state or a ない-form, because it describes something that you do not control directly. 忘れないように紙に書きます means “I write it on paper so that I do not forget”. 見えるように窓を洗います means “I wash the window so that it can be seen”.",[["忘れない","wasurenai","do not forget"],["ように","youni","so that"],["、","",""],["紙","kami","paper"],["に","ni","on"],["書きます","kakimasu","write"],["。","",""]],"I write it on paper so that I do not forget."]
];
